    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Niemann-Pick type C disease is a rare fatal lysosomal storage disease that causes progressive neurodegeneration due to mutations in the npc1 gene. However, therapeutic treatments for this disease have not been found. One approach to identifying therapeutic targets is to screen for protein products that either suppress NPC1 levels or change cholesterol metabolism. Drosophila melanogaster with loss of function of npc1a, a homolog of NPC1, exhibit aberrant cholesterol accumulation similar to NPC-diseased mammalian tissue. The npc1a mutants have impaired synthesis of the steroid hormone ecdysone, which is required for molting during transitions between the larval stages of fly development. We used a sensitized RNAi screen in which npc1a is inhibited in the Drosophila ring gland, the neuroendocrine gland that produces ecdysone. Providing cholesterol precursors to the fly larvae in their food rescues the larvae to adulthood. By titrating the cholesterol amounts, we were able to use this RNAi screen to identify genes that suppressed the mutant npc1a phenotype. We screened a set of candidate genes whose protein products are involved in trafficking, sterol metabolism, and cell death functions.\r\nWe found that genes that suppressed the NPC phenotype in flies and human cells are involved with two cellular processes: early autophagy and endoplasmic reticulum-Golgi communication. Using the information from this initial screening, further testing can be done on other proteins involved in those processes to delineate the membrane trafficking events and mechanisms that underlie the NPC phenotype.  The information from the screen and further analyses may be useful for understanding NPC disease and in designing new treatment strategies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
